  "anos": "From the First World War (1914) to today.",
  "abre": "<p>The <strong>First World War</strong> (1914-1918), with millions dead in the trenches and weapons made in series, shatters the 19th century’s confidence in progress. In 1917, the Russian Revolution promises a classless society. The century begins by asking what went wrong in the civilisation that believed itself the most advanced.</p>",
  "social": "<p>The <strong>mass society</strong> arrives: big cities, consumption, cinema, radio and television. Women win the vote (in Spain, in 1931) and schooling reaches almost everyone. In the second half of the century the <strong>welfare state</strong> spreads across Europe, with public healthcare and pensions.</p>\n<p>Today we live in a <strong>globalised</strong> world connected by the internet, with enormous inequalities, great migrations and an ecological crisis that forces us to rethink progress.</p>",
  "politico": "<p>Between the two world wars <strong>totalitarianisms</strong> emerge (fascism, Nazism, Stalinism). The Second World War (1939-1945) ends with the Holocaust and with the atomic bombs of Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Afterwards the UN and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) are born, the Cold War begins, and Asia and Africa are decolonised.</p>\n<p>In Spain, the Francoist dictatorship (1939-1975) sends many thinkers into exile, such as María Zambrano. The fall of the Berlin Wall (1989) closes the Cold War; the 21st century brings the financial crisis of 2008, the covid-19 pandemic (2020) and new wars.</p>",
  "ciencia": "<p>Einstein (relativity) and quantum mechanics change the idea of space, time and matter. In 1953 the structure of DNA is described, with data from Rosalind Franklin. Turing (1936) and Shannon (1948) lay the foundations of computing. Nuclear energy, the journey to the Moon (1969), the internet and artificial intelligence show an enormous technical power, which forces us to ask about its limits.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap diag\"><figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 480 154\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An expanding universe: It had a beginning, about 13,800 million years ago, and it is expanding. Space and time curve; in the very small, all is probable.\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><title>An expanding universe: It had a beginning, about 13,800 million years ago, and it is expanding. Space and time curve; in the very small, all is probable.</title><defs><marker id=\"flecon2\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-mk\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"4\" y=\"6\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"34\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"56\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/><ellipse cx=\"92.9\" cy=\"69.8\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(20 92.9 69.8)\"/><path d=\"M117.3 61.0 L132.4 55.5\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#flecon2)\"/><ellipse cx=\"72.2\" cy=\"54.3\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(100 72.2 54.3)\"/><path d=\"M68.4 32.7 L65.6 16.9\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#flecon2)\"/><ellipse cx=\"62.8\" cy=\"71.2\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(160 62.8 71.2)\"/><path d=\"M34.7 61.0 L19.6 55.5\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#flecon2)\"/><ellipse cx=\"60.6\" cy=\"94.4\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(230 60.6 94.4)\"/><path d=\"M47.7 109.7 L37.4 122.0\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#flecon2)\"/><ellipse cx=\"86.0\" cy=\"93.3\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(300 86.0 93.3)\"/><path d=\"M98.0 114.1 L106.0 128.0\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#flecon2)\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-o\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"34\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">An expanding universe</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"54\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">20th-21st c.</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"80\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">It had a beginning, about</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"99\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">13,800 million years ago, and it is</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"118\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">expanding. Space and time curve;</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"137\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">in the very small, all is probable.</text></svg></figure></div>\n<p><strong>What was called “science”?</strong> Theories that can be tested and corrected (Popper), developed and reviewed by a scientific community (Kuhn). Science grows as never before, but becomes humbler: quantum physics shows that there are limits to what we can understand.</p>",
  "arte": "<p>The <strong>avant-gardes</strong> break with tradition: the Cubism of Picasso and Braque breaks forms down from 1907 onwards; Dadaism is born in Zurich in 1916 as a mockery of a civilisation that has produced the war; and Marcel Duchamp presents a urinal with the title <em>Fountain</em> (1917). Since then, the question ‘what is art?’ has been part of art. Surrealism (Breton’s manifesto, 1924) seeks the unconscious that Freud had described. Picasso’s <em>Guernica</em> (1937) denounces the bombing of the town, and that same year the Nazis exhibit modern art as ‘degenerate art’.</p>\n<p>Technology changes art. Walter Benjamin wrote in 1936 that mass reproduction, such as photography and cinema, makes the work lose its ‘aura’ as a unique piece; Adorno and Horkheimer criticised the <strong>culture industry</strong>, which manufactures entertainment as a commodity. When Andy Warhol exhibited his <em>Brillo</em> scouring-pad boxes (1964), the philosopher Arthur Danto concluded that what makes an object art is not its appearance, but a theory and an ‘artworld’ that recognises it. Today the debate continues with digital art and images made with artificial intelligence: who is the author?</p>",
  "pensamiento": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Psychoanalysis:</strong> Freud shows that we are not fully masters of our own minds.</li>\n<li><strong>Existence and phenomenology:</strong> Heidegger, Sartre, Beauvoir and Camus start from concrete existence, freedom and anguish.</li>\n<li><strong>Linguistic turn:</strong> Wittgenstein and analytic philosophy examine language; Popper and Kuhn, science.</li>\n<li><strong>Critical theory:</strong> the Frankfurt School (Horkheimer, Adorno, Habermas) analyses mass society.</li>\n<li><strong>Philosophy in Spain:</strong> Unamuno, Ortega and Zambrano; in Basque, Txillardegi and Azurmendi.</li>\n<li><strong>Debates today:</strong> feminism (Beauvoir, Butler), postmodernity (Lyotard, Foucault, Derrida), justice (Rawls), ecology (Yayo Herrero), technology and mind (Han, Bostrom, Chalmers).</li>\n</ul>",
  "rupturas": "<p>It is an era that has not ended, with several internal breaks. Auschwitz and Hiroshima force us to ask what reason has done (Adorno, Arendt); in 1979, Lyotard announces the end of the ‘grand narratives’. The questions open today, such as artificial intelligence, the climate crisis or disinformation, may already be opening another era that we do not yet know how to name.</p>",
  "trampa": "<p>‘In today’s philosophy anything goes.’ That there are many currents does not mean that any opinion will do: each argues with the others using reasons. And ‘postmodern’ is not an insult, nor does it mean ‘very modern’: it is a specific thesis about the end of the grand narratives, which also has critics, such as Habermas.</p>"
